SIIS - REMAINING FEATURES
=========================

This file tracks features that are not yet fully implemented or are intentionally
deferred until the web platform is complete.

1. MOBILE APP CONVERSION
------------------------

- Convert the completed React web application into an Android application using
  Capacitor.
- Add the required Capacitor Android project and native build configuration.
- Configure the application ID, app name, launcher icon, splash screen, signing
  configuration, and release build settings.
- Produce and test a debug APK, release APK, and, if required, an Android App
  Bundle (AAB).
- Test installation, startup, upgrades, and offline/reconnect behavior on real
  Android devices.

2. CAMERA-ONLY REPORT UPLOAD
----------------------------

- Add an Android camera capture flow for reporting an issue.
- The report screen should allow a user to capture a new image directly from the
  device camera.
- Decide whether gallery/file uploads should remain available. The requested
  target behavior is camera-only upload for the mobile app.
- Request and explain camera permission before opening the camera.
- Handle denied, cancelled, unavailable, and interrupted camera operations.
- Validate captured image type, dimensions, and file size before submission.
- Preserve the existing image preview, AI detection, annotation, and report
  submission workflow after capture.

3. DEVICE LOCATION AND GPS
--------------------------

- Integrate Capacitor Geolocation for mobile devices.
- Request location permission with a clear explanation.
- Use the device location when submitting a report.
- Allow the user to retry location acquisition when permission is denied or GPS
  is unavailable.
- Keep the default map center fallback for users who do not share their location.
- Clearly distinguish a default map center from the user's actual position.
- Test location behavior indoors, with GPS disabled, and with approximate
  location enabled.

4. MOBILE MAP EXPERIENCE
------------------------

- Validate Leaflet rendering inside the Android WebView.
- Ensure marker icons, marker legends, popups, tile loading, and fit-to-bounds
  work on small screens.
- Add an appropriate loading and error state when map tiles or nearby data are
  unavailable.
- Consider a native-map implementation only if WebView Leaflet performance is
  insufficient.

5. PUSH NOTIFICATIONS
---------------------

- Add push notifications for report status changes such as acknowledged,
  under review, resolved, or rejected.
- Register mobile device tokens securely.
- Add notification preferences and a way to disable non-essential notifications.
- Deep-link a notification to the relevant report details screen.

6. OFFLINE AND RETRY SUPPORT
----------------------------

- Detect loss of connectivity before report submission.
- Keep a captured report locally when upload cannot complete.
- Show pending, retrying, failed, and submitted states.
- Retry uploads without creating duplicate reports.
- Remove local image/report data after confirmed server persistence.
- Protect locally stored report data and captured images.

7. AUTHENTICATION HARDENING
---------------------------

- Replace localStorage JWT persistence with a more secure mobile/web session
  strategy where appropriate.
- Add token expiry handling and refresh/re-login behavior.
- Add logout from all active devices if required.
- Add password reset and account recovery.
- Add profile editing and password change flows.

8. ADMIN AND OPERATIONS IMPROVEMENTS
------------------------------------

- Add pagination to admin and community report lists.
- Add bulk status updates for administrators.
- Add export of report and issue-group data.
- Add audit information showing the administrator who changed a status.
- Add charts and trends for issue types, severity, priority, and resolution time.
- Add admin controls for notification delivery and model availability.

9. DATA AND SECURITY HARDENING
------------------------------

- Sanitize public report responses so unnecessary ownership fields are not
  exposed.
- Validate JWT_SECRET and other required secrets at backend startup.
- Review Supabase RLS policies and service-role usage.
- Add rate limiting for authentication, detection, and report submission routes.
- Add server-side input validation and request size limits to every relevant
  endpoint.
- Add structured logging and monitoring without logging passwords, tokens, or
  private image contents.

10. AI AND MODEL MANAGEMENT
---------------------------

- Add model version metadata to each detection/report.
- Add an administrator-visible model health and version page.
- Add configurable confidence thresholds per model if needed.
- Add automated regression samples for pothole, road-crack, broken-bin,
  overflowing-bin, and trash-on-road detections.
- Verify that the native trash model labels remain unchanged:
  broken_bin, overflowing_bin, and trash_on_road.
- Add model loading and inference performance monitoring.

11. TESTING AND RELEASE READINESS
---------------------------------

- Add backend integration tests for authentication, report persistence, grouping,
  nearby reports, status updates, and notifications.
- Add AI-service tests for all detector outputs and invalid images.
- Add frontend tests for route guards, mobile navigation, loading states, filters,
  maps, and report submission.
- Run accessibility checks on desktop and mobile layouts.
- Test the complete workflow on supported browsers and Android versions.
- Add CI checks for linting, builds, database migration validation, and tests.
- Document environment variables, local startup, deployment, and rollback steps.

12. DEPLOYMENT
--------------

- Deploy the frontend, Express backend, and FastAPI AI service independently or
  using a documented combined deployment.
- Configure HTTPS, production CORS origins, secure secrets, SMTP, Supabase, and
  model file storage.
- Configure production logging, health checks, backups, and monitoring.
- Publish the Android application through the intended distribution channel.

Priority order:
1. Camera-only capture and mobile location.
2. Capacitor Android conversion and real-device testing.
3. Offline/retry and push notifications.
4. Security hardening and automated tests.
5. Production deployment and store/distribution release.
